{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on revised direction 279bb63f. Initial acquisition is now sound: write a complete private file and link it into the owner pathname, so exactly one fresh starter wins and no partial claim is visible. Demoting resident.json to endpoint metadata behind one separate ownership authority is also the correct split. Refusing on every ambiguous probe, binding before contest but never serving before ownership, nonce-and-inode guarded withdrawal, and a bounded side-effect-free loopback identity endpoint returning only genesis are accepted. One stale-transfer interleaving is still missing and breaks exclusivity. Taker A and taker B both read and definitively probe the old owner as dead. B pauses. A renames the old owner to its tombstone and links a fresh claim at owner. B resumes and calls rename(owner, owner.dead.B). Pathname rename is not conditional on the inode B probed: it succeeds against A new live claim instead of returning ENOENT, moves that claim away, and lets B acquire. The same issue exists if an ordinary newcomer links between A rename and B delayed rename. Therefore rename-to-our-nonce is not compare-and-swap, and section 4 claim that the second rename must see ENOENT is false once the pathname has been repopulated. Revise stale transfer around a primitive that conditionally owns the observed generation. One viable file-only shape is to hard-link the observed old owner into a deterministic transfer name keyed by the observed owner nonce, so only one taker wins that transfer, verify the owner and transfer link still name the same inode before the sole winner unlinks, then let fresh link acquisition arbitrate any newcomer; specify crash and pre-existing-tombstone recovery. A Git ref update with expected old value, an advisory lock within the supported platform envelope, or another proven CAS is also acceptable. The protocol must include a mutation-sensitive test for the exact delayed-second-taker/new-owner interleaving. Architecture: separate owner authority plus advertisement metadata preserves Git CAS and the process-local Nexus contract while changing ordinary Git storage, application-host lifecycle, service identity, and gs serving; accepted except for stale-transfer atomicity. Security: strict loopback validation, bounded credential-free GET, no CORS authority, and fail-closed mismatches are sound; deleting a live winner through the stale-transfer race remains a correctness and availability defect. Simplification: shared Nexus remains fluff and generation is correctly cut; link acquisition is suitably small, but rename is not a CAS and cannot be kept merely for surface simplicity. No phase-2 implementation is authorized until this race is closed.","body":{"proposal":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:279bb63fbfc510acc9b29fefbd5326f2e6d13f00","verdict":"changes-requested"}}